{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"**Verdict: gate fail.** F2’s custody change resolves the reported transport and version-2 cases, but F1 still loses occurrence provenance, and the new batch claim path introduces validation and conditional-creation defects.\n\n1. **The same tree loaded twice still loses the first table.** [macro.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1432) keys tables solely by final root address. Two expansions can produce the same root while their rows have different source-batch origins. The second `assoc-in` overwrites the first, and `call-site-table` cannot distinguish the two executions from their identical packets. Similarly, an unclaimed load of a root previously loaded with a claim can retrieve the old table. The new regression explicitly uses *different* roots, so it does not cover either case. Custody needs an occurrence identity, including the admission origin, in addition to content identity.\n\n2. **Validation stops after the first claiming member.** In [encoder.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/encoder.cljc:325), `some` short-circuits the lazy sequence of `check-call-site-claim!` calls. A valid claiming member followed by a member with incomplete coverage or an unsupported marker version can therefore be encoded without checking the latter. The previous `doseq` checked every member.\n\n3. **A claim on a different member creates metadata for an unclaimed run member.** The same `some` collapses all member verdicts into one batch boolean. [expand-batch](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1299) uses that boolean to finalize metadata for `run-index`. Thus a batch with a claimed declaration member and an unmarked selected member manufactures a table for the latter. The conditional needs to reflect the selected member’s claim while still validating every member.\n\nOn the remaining review points:\n\n- **F2:** The manifest references the table and record, the closure walk reads both, and the reverse record does not name the manifest. The publisher rejects version `2` at the claim. One smaller diagnostic issue remains: `nil` or `false` marker values are refused as incomplete coverage rather than with the specified unsupported-version reason, because the dispatch tests the value’s truthiness in [publish.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:233).\n- **Boundary:** The encoder and closure changes are justified: they are respectively the marker-loss boundary and the custody traversal point.\n- **Compatibility and neighbor test:** The expander accepts existing five-element batches as unclaimed, and the comparison of their first five elements is sound because datom conversion drops occurrence metadata. Static inspection found no changed oracle site-table path.\n- The reported remote and local results are useful evidence for the covered cases. This was a static review; I did not independently rerun the suites."}}
